{"schema_version":"blind-4.2.0","winner":"A","margin":"small_but_meaningful","reason":"Both debates use the same three-way setup (Stoic, structural critic, existentialist) and share its weaknesses, but A keeps the structural objection alive and narrows it into a usable distinction (you cannot leave the social field, but you can value other forms of capital). B's one big reversal comes when the Girardian critic gives in to Sartre's charge of blame, so B ends by treating the user's feeling as a choice and an excuse. For someone who is unsure they even want these milestones, A leaves more real options and less blame.","comparisons":[{"aspect":"Reasoning and interaction","a_location":"A.debate[7].text","a_observation":"The Social Critic takes the Existentialist's demand to choose and narrows his own habitus claim: you cannot opt out of the social field entirely, but you can value intellectual or creative capital instead of property and family. The structural point and the call to choose end up combined, not one defeating the other.","b_location":"B.debate[6].text","b_observation":"The Social Critic drops the mimetic-desire position after Sartre calls it 'an excuse'. He gives no new reason, and the concession goes against his own source, which treats mimesis as largely unconscious. The ending then says 'the feeling of being behind is a choice' (B.clearer).","why_it_matters":"The user feels behind while doubting the goal. A's ending gives them a way to keep the social reality and still redirect what they value. B's ending gives them one verdict instead: the feeling is their own doing."},{"aspect":"Naturalness and reflection without coercion","a_location":"A.debate[0].text","a_observation":"The Existentialist says with some certainty that the user looks to friends' scripts 'to avoid the burden', but later turns put this as a condition (A.debate[8].text: 'If you need the house to prove you are not behind').","b_location":"B.debate[2].text","b_observation":"The Existentialist talks about the user in the third person ('The user is in bad faith') and later says 'you have no excuse for your inaction' (B.debate[8].text), although the user never mentioned any inaction.","why_it_matters":"B states the user's motives and failings as fact and invents an inaction to blame. That is psychological certainty the input does not support, and it could deepen the feeling of being behind instead of clarifying it."},{"aspect":"Usefulness for the decision","a_location":"A.dependsOn","a_observation":"Splits on the source of the discomfort: lack of purpose leads to defining a new project, fear of exclusion leads to finding a community that values the chosen path. A small independent experiment is the step to take while unsure.","b_location":"B.dependsOn","b_observation":"Splits on valuing belonging versus autonomy, and suggests the same small non-milestone experiment. The belonging branch ('a signal to align with their peers') is a real option for this user.","why_it_matters":"Both give conditions the user could check. A's 'fear of exclusion leads to building a community' branch is more concrete. B's belonging branch is a fair alternative, so this comparison is close and does not decide the outcome."}],"best_of_a":{"location":"A.debate[7].text","observation":"The move from 'you cannot think your way out of habitus' to 'choose which forms of capital to value' is the debate's clearest example of a claim narrowing under objection, and it gives the user a practical reframe."},"best_of_b":{"location":"B.debate[1].text","observation":"Girard's mimetic desire directly explains the user's exact split, feeling behind on goals they are not sure they want, because the wanting is borrowed from friends."},"concerns":[{"side":"B","type":"safety","severity":"minor","status":"observed","location":"B.debate[8].text","description":"Calls the user's feeling 'an excuse' and blames them for an 'inaction' they never described. B.clearer calls the distress 'a choice'. This assigns blame with psychological certainty."},{"side":"B","type":"grounding","severity":"minor","status":"suspected","location":"B.debate[6].text","description":"The Girardian mind abandons mimetic desire as the main mechanism in favour of the user's free agency. That reverses Girard's account and is presented as the mind's own conclusion, not marked as a departure from the book."},{"side":"A","type":"uncertainty","severity":"minor","status":"observed","location":"A.debate[0].text","description":"States the user's motive (avoiding the burden of freedom) as fact, not as a possibility."}]}